[[File:Michelangelo Valentino.jpg|thumb|302x302px|[[Michelangelo Valentino]], an early proponent of Baliscan nationalism]]
'''Baliscan nationalism''' asserts that [[Baliscans]] form a [[W:Nation|nation]] and should promote the cultural unity of Baliscans of all ethnic, linguistic and religious backgrounds. Baliscan nationalism is a form of [[W:Territorial nationalism|territorial nationalism]] and emphasizes a cultural connection of the people to the land. It first emerged in the early 1700s following the dissolution of the [[Andalusian Empire]] as a result of the [[War of the Alpujarras]]. It was founded on the belief in the necessity for the people living in the [[Baliscan archipelago]] of multiple backgrounds to unite as one people to be able to resist colonial [[Reconquiesta|Andalusian reconquest]]. The nationalists' dream of an unified state spanning the [[Northern Cone]] was achieved in 1883 when the [[Maroon Confederacy]] joined the confederation as the [[Araucaria|Republic of Araucaria]]. Substantial immigration in the post-civil war years resulted in the development of a [[W:Cosmopolitanism|cosmopolitan]] and [[W:Multiculturalism|multicultural]] Baliscan society, which would ultimately produce a [[W:Civic nationalism|liberal]] form of nationalism, ''[[Baliscanismo]]''.

Baliscan nationalism has been negatively affected by multiple historical episodes of ethnic violence and repression of certain ethnic groups by both colonial and post-colonial governments between the various peoples has resulted in multiple [[W:Secession|secessionist]] movements demanding dissolution of the confederation. Most notably was the [[Xunta Nacionalista]], which withdrew from the confederation in 1828 (sparking the [[Baliscan Civil War]]) and embarked on a concerted campaign of violent [[Maroon removal]] across north-central [[Abyala]].

Baliscan nationalism declined in the early 20th century, in part replaced by the militant [[W:Proletarian internationalism|proletarian internationalism]] and [[W:Anti-patriotism|anti-patriotism]] of the [[Baliscan Revolution|Revolutionary period]]. In the 21st century, Baliscan nationalism has experienced a resurgence, largely in the form of [[W:Left-wing nationalism|left-wing nationalist]] and [[W:Cultural conservatism|cultural conservative]] sentiment and rhetoric.

